---
description: Write project docs as terse facts in headings, tables, lists, code, and alerts.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Terse documentation

When writing or editing `docs/`, `README.md`, or other project docs, state the facts and stop. Apply this style to the text you add or replace. Leave the surrounding text as it is.

Keep every fact the reader needs to act. Drop restatement and implications that follow from the fact.

## Shape

| Form | Use for |
| --- | --- |
| Heading | One topic |
| Table | Records that share the same fields |
| Unordered list | A set of peers |
| Ordered list | Steps, in order |
| Task list | A checklist in a short procedure |
| Fenced code | Commands, config, paths, filenames, samples |
| Alert | One hard constraint (`> [!WARNING]` or `> [!IMPORTANT]`) |

Prefer a table, list, code block, or alert. Prose is allowed when those forms do not fit: one or two sentences under the heading. Do not add a table, list, code block, or alert that carries no fact of its own.

Keep lists flat. Requirement state stays in the status table (`Done` / `Open`).

## Omit

- Why a fact matters when the fact already says it.
- A second sentence that repeats the first.
- Lead-ins ("Note that", "It is important", "In other words").
- "What this means" after the instruction.

## Example

Oppressive:

> AUTH_SECRET, keeper username, and site title stay in the container environment. The keeper password hash lives in the vault with the photographs, so a volume clone keeps the same key. Existing sessions use AUTH_SECRET; a new secret only invalidates cookies, not the password. The public library comes up from the volume alone.
>
> Do not copy /data while the container is writing. docker compose stop first. Schedule that archive; the volume is the gallery.

Terse:

| Item | Stored in |
| --- | --- |
| `AUTH_SECRET` | Container environment. A new value invalidates cookies only. |
| Keeper username, site title | Container environment |
| Keeper password hash, photographs | `/data` |

> [!WARNING]
> `docker compose stop`, then copy `/data`. Restore the gallery from that copy.
